524	== Specification/appendices/changes.tex:1358-1552
525	
526	\subsection{The traits that extend a closed trait}
527	\seclabel{revival-comprises}
528	
529	\begin{description}
530	\item[Affected sections] \secref{trait-decls}; \secref{natparams}, by the
531	amendment of 9~October 2026.
532	\item[Change] The first sentence of a draft note in \secref{trait-decls},
533	which said that the traits listed in a \KWD{comprises} clause are exactly
534	the traits that immediately extend the declared trait and that they must
535	explicitly extend it, is replaced by normative text: every listed type
536	extends the declared trait \VAR{T}, and every value of type \VAR{T} is a
537	value of one of the listed types; a listed type may be an instantiation of
538	a parameterized trait whose static arguments contain no static variable
539	other than the static parameters of \VAR{T}; and a trait or object that explicitly extends
540	\VAR{T} is a subtype of a listed type, or a trait with a \KWD{comprises}
541	clause of its own each of whose listed types meets the same requirement, or
542	a trait with static parameters that at least one trait or object extends
543	and every trait or object extending it is a subtype of a listed type.
544	The note's second sentence, on a clause that includes ``\EXP{\ldots}'', is
545	kept in its note, and the example of \TYP{Molecule} is unchanged.
546	The new text answers the third question of Victor Luchangco's draft note
547	on trait identifiers earlier in the section, whether an instantiation of a
548	parameterized trait may be listed: it may.
549	An amendment of 9~October 2026 lets the static arguments of a listed type
550	contain a \KWD{where}-clause variable of the declaration of \VAR{T} as well,
551	the listed type then holding at every value of the variable, and adds one
552	sentence to \secref{natparams}: an argument whose type is a trait whose clause
553	lists a type at a \KWD{nat} or \KWD{int} \KWD{where}-clause variable, passed
554	where that listed type is expected with a \KWD{nat} or \KWD{int} parameter in
555	the variable's place, instantiates that parameter, for that call, with the
556	size of the value itself, a size known to equal only itself.
557	\item[Rationale] This change does not follow from route~A; the revival
558	decided it on 28~September 2026.
559	The team's later Types chapter (Luchangco, 2012) reads a \KWD{comprises}
560	clause as coverage: the clause ``specifies a set of types and generic types
561	determined by the generic type of the declaration'', and an instantiation of
562	the declared generic type ``is covered by the union of the types in this set
563	and the corresponding instantiations of the generic type in this set'', one
564	generic type $G$ determining another $G'$ ``if every parameter of $G'$ is a
565	parameter of $G$''
566	(\nolinkurl{Documentation/Specification/Prose/Language/types.tick}, lines
567	277--283 and 384--390, in the revival's repository).
568	Welterweight Fortress, the calculus of the type group's paper of 2012
569	(Chase, Hilburn, Luchangco, Naden, Ryu, Steele, and Tristan, ``Dynamic
570	Dispatch and Type Inference Semipredicates''), reads it at the level of
571	values: ``no value can belong to the trait unless it also belongs to one of
572	the comprised types''; its rule for a trait declaration asks nothing of the
573	traits that extend a trait with such a clause
574	(\nolinkurl{Papers/Welterweight/grammar.tick}, lines 21--22;
575	\nolinkurl{Papers/Welterweight/fig-wellformeddecls.tick}, lines 38--62).
576	The draft note restated the rule of the compiled type checker of 2009,
577	under which \library\ could not close their marker of the integer types as
578	the team meant to: \TYP{AnyIntegral} lists the five integer types, and the
579	parameterized trait \EXP{\TYP{Integral}\llbracket{}I\rrbracket}, which each
580	of them extends at itself, stands between them unlisted, as the team's
581	comment on the declaration records (``not yet: comprises
582	\EXP{\TYP{Integral}\llbracket{}I\rrbracket} where
583	\EXP{\llbracket{}I\rrbracket}'', \nolinkurl{Library/FortressLibrary.fsi},
584	line 434).
585	\TYP{Integral} has a static parameter that \TYP{AnyIntegral} does not have,
586	so it can be listed only at particular static arguments, and every value of it is a value of one of the five.
587	The full reasoning is in
588	\nolinkurl{explorations/compile-ladder/rung-spec-comprises/decision-record.md}
589	in the revival's repository.
590	The amendment follows the revival's default for the third question of its
591	array design, a size known only at run time, built on 9~October 2026 and
592	listed for the curator's review.
593	\library\ have carried on their trait \TYP{NatParam}, since 2007, the clause
594	``\EXP{\KWD{comprises}\;\{N\llbracket{}n\rrbracket\}\;\KWD{where}\;\llbracket\KWD{nat}\;n\rrbracket}''
595	as a comment, with the reading that a function taking an
596	\EXP{N\llbracket{}n\rrbracket} may be passed a \TYP{NatParam}, and ``within
597	that function n becomes a static nat parameter''
598	(\nolinkurl{ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/NatReflect.fss}, lines 15--26, in
599	the revival's repository).
600	The sentence of 28~September followed the Types chapter's rule by which one
601	generic type determines another, which does not consider \KWD{where}-clause
602	variables; \secref{where-clauses} allows a declaration to introduce them.
603	A size so opened is bound for the call, as inference binds a type parameter
604	to an argument's type, and since no program can write its name it cannot be
605	equal to a size written elsewhere.
606	The team wrote the same form at a type \KWD{where}-clause variable as a comment
607	on \TYP{AnyList} (``Not yet: comprises
608	\EXP{\TYP{List}\llbracket{}E\rrbracket} where \EXP{\llbracket{}E\rrbracket}'',
609	\nolinkurl{Library/List.fsi}, line 56, in the revival's repository); the
610	revival decided on 16~October 2026 to build the forms of \KWD{where} clause the
611	team wrote down, that comment among them.
612	The full reasoning of the amendment is in
613	\nolinkurl{explorations/compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md} and
614	\nolinkurl{explorations/reviews/array-fork3-judgement.md} in the revival's
615	repository.
616	\item[Effect] The compiled type checker accepts the declaration of
617	\TYP{AnyIntegral} in \library\ as written
618	(\nolinkurl{Library/FortressLibrary.fsi}, lines 433--436).
619	No example of this specification changes: \TYP{ExclusiveMolecule}, which
620	has no static parameters, is still not allowed.
621	The compiled type checker enforces the requirement on a trait with static
622	parameters over the declarations and object expressions it sees: a trait or object declared in
623	another component, below such a trait and a subtype of no listed type, is
624	not refused (row~487 of the revival's gap ledger). It compares the declarations it sees by their names: for a parameterized \VAR{T} its verdict can depend on how an extender spells its static parameters, and a trait or object that extends a trait of the same simple name in another API counts as an extender (rows~489 and~490).
625	It refuses an object expression that extends a trait with a \KWD{comprises}
626	clause, explicitly or through the traits it extends, and is a subtype of no
627	listed type, as the requirement on the trait's extenders together demands of
628	it, an object expression having neither static parameters nor a clause of its
629	own; before 3~October 2026 it read no object expression against a clause
630	(row~597).
631	The interpreter checks at load the traits and objects that explicitly extend a
632	trait with a \KWD{comprises} clause declared in the component it executes, and
633	refuses one that is neither a subtype of a listed type nor another of the
634	extenders that \secref{trait-decls} allows.
635	It does not yet check a clause declared in another component, of the program
636	or of \library\ (rows~22, 487, 551, 595 and~596), an object expression that
637	extends such a trait (row~597), or that each listed type extends \VAR{T}
638	(row~598).
