640	\KWD{where}-clause variable as a size, reads a listed type at every value of
641	such a variable when it checks the traits and objects that extend \VAR{T}, and
642	opens an argument as the new sentence of \secref{natparams} says, so that the
643	value of \VAR{reflect} in \library\ is accepted where an
644	\EXP{N\llbracket{}n\rrbracket} is expected; it refuses an opened size that
645	reaches a type naming another size, and gives two values opened in one call
646	two sizes, so it refuses a call that needs them equal where the interpreter
647	runs it when they are.
648	It reads a declaration whose parameter type is such a trait and a declaration
649	with a \KWD{nat} parameter whose parameter type is the listed type at it as
650	applicable to the same calls, and refuses the pair as duplicates, where the
651	interpreter runs it and calls the second; the text does not say whether the
652	opening counts for the rules of \secref{overloading-static-params}
